<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalb Ali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1887</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> Ali Khan was Nawab of Rampur in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1865 to 1887. A poet and scholar of Arabic and Persian, he enlarged the state library that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raza-library">Raza Library</a> and made his court a refuge for poets and musicians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> poet Dagh Dehlvi. He remained a loyal ally of the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalb-ali-khan.jpg" alt="Kalb Ali Khan" width="320" height="557" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bourne and Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Kalb_Ali_Khan,_Nawab_of_Rampur_(1832-1887).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
